**Deadlock.** A set of processes each hold a resource and wait for one held by another, so none can proceed. It requires all four Coffman conditions: **mutual exclusion**, **hold and wait**, **no preemption**, **circular wait**. Handling it:

- **Prevention**: break one condition, e.g. acquire all locks at once, or impose a global lock ordering to prevent circular wait.
- **Avoidance**: grant a request only if the system stays in a safe state (Banker's algorithm).
- **Detection and recovery**: find cycles in the wait-for graph, then kill or roll back a process.

**Semaphore.** An integer with atomic `wait(P)` (decrement, block if it would go negative) and `signal(V)` (increment, wake a waiter). A counting semaphore limits access to N identical resources; a binary semaphore (0 or 1) provides mutual exclusion. Unlike a mutex, a semaphore has no owner and can be signalled by another thread.

**Synchronization.** Coordinating threads that share data so results do not depend on timing (race conditions). The critical section must have mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting. Tools: mutexes, semaphores, monitors, condition variables.

**Paging.** Physical memory is split into fixed-size frames and the logical address space into pages of the same size. A **page table** maps page numbers to frames, with a TLB caching recent translations. It avoids external fragmentation.

**Virtual memory.** Gives each process a large private address space while only part is in RAM. Pages are loaded on demand; a **page fault** occurs when an accessed page is not in memory, and the OS fetches it from disk, possibly evicting another page (LRU, FIFO). Benefits: process isolation, programs larger than RAM, and simpler loading.

**Middle of a linked list:**

```java
Node mid(Node head) {
    Node slow = head, fast = head;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;
    }
    return slow; // second middle for even length
}
```

Time O(n), space O(1).
